# Cockles and Mussels (Molly Malone) - Iers lied
# Volgens Wikipedia (en), "Molly Malone": eerste druk 1876 in Boston; een Londense
# uitgave van 1884 noemt James Yorkston uit Edinburgh als auteur. Ouder dan 1900
# en daarmee publiek domein.
# Overgenomen uit COCKLES.MUS (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991). Twee regels die in de bron aan
# elkaar stonden zijn gesplitst. Akkoorden alleen bij het eerste couplet. Het
# refrein staat in de bron één keer; hier na elk couplet.
{title: Cockles and Mussels}
{meta: id cockles-and-mussels}
{meta: language en}
{lyricist: James Yorkston}
{composer: James Yorkston}
{year: 1876}
{copyright: Public domain}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: source Overgenomen uit COCKLES.MUS (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991); auteur en oudste druk via Wikipedia (en)}
{meta: tag volkslied}
{meta: tag kampvuur}
{key: C}

{start_of_verse}
In Dub[C]lin's fair city, [Am]where girl[Dm]s are so pretty,[G7]
I [C]first set my [A7]eyes on sweet Mol[D7]ly Malon[G7]e
As she wheeled [C]her wheel-barrow, throug[Am]h streets broad and narr[Dm]ow,[G7]
crying : "Coc[C]kles and [F]mussel[C]s, alive[F], [C]alive [G7]oh [C]!"
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Alive alive-o, alive alive-o
crying : "Cockles and mussels, alive, alive oh !"
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse}
She was a fishmonger, and sure 't was no wonder,
for so were her father and mother before
They each pushed their wheel-barrow, through streets broad and narrow,
crying : "Cockles and mussels, alive, alive oh !"
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
She died of a fever, and no one could save her
and that was the end of sweet Molly Malone
Her ghost wheels her barrow, through streets broad and narrow,
crying : "Cockles and mussels, alive, alive oh !"
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
